Understand differences between dynamic and static responses. 
If everyone gets the same page, that means everyone’s getting the same page, that’s a static page.
Say if you make a request to the signing in to your account, that’s a dynamic response. Responding to a customized request. 

Understand why you’re getting request header, request body. 
If the server needs to send you a response, it will send a response header. 
A request header is followed by a request body. 
If cookies need to be sent, they’ll be sent inside a header field. 

When the server sends back the request, there’s also a thing called “e-tag” to determine whether the content has been changed or not.  

A situation when the response does not contain any resource (response body)? If the only part of the response is just the header.
HEAD method: Only requesting the header, not the body (when you’re only wanting to access meta data)

Anything that can be customized for the user, cookie communication between the user and server, that’s the dynamic web-page behaviour (Instagram, Brightspace).

Design pattern: MVC (Model-View-Controller)
-	Model: responsible for the data, anything related to database system interaction. 
-	View: responsible for the presentation of how the web page might look like. Basically the UI. 
-	Controller: an intermediary between Model and View, controls what gets sent from the view.

